=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: policy
rules: delegate, background, store, pr-wait
intent: Translation: workers spawn with async `delegate_task`; the Project tracker and Project store become a `notes.md` tracker in the shared per-repo pstack state folder; PR and CI event subscriptions become `watch_pull_request`, with a schedule_task heartbeat for merges and unwatching before handback (rule pr-wait). Policy (change reload-the-orchestrator-after): the coordinator also keeps `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` current, because it is the one file that survives compaction and the compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back. Upstream dropped drains and standing orders, so the file is rewritten whenever the program's state changes and holds the goal instead of the standing orders. Preserve no scripts, no polling loops, and no relaying between agents.
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task` (`mode: "async"`), and keep each `taskId` and `childThreadId`. Keep the program's state in a `notes.md` tracker in the store, `~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"` (create it if missing). Also keep `$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` current. It is the one file that survives compaction, so it lives in the project and not the store. Rewrite it whenever the program's state changes, under 6,000 characters. It holds the goal, the live agents with their `taskId`s, the Pods, the open PRs, the decisions waiting on the user, and the next steps. The compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back; install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read. Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request`, which wakes you on CI results, reviews, and conflicts, with a `schedule_task` heartbeat of 20 to 30 minutes, because a merge ends the watch without a wake. Call `unwatch_pull_request` and `delete_scheduled_task` before handing the program back. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, transcripts, model-effort, resume, stop, same-model-review
intent: Workers default to their own worktree the way upstream defaults them to a cloud VM, and the list of reasons to stay on this machine becomes a note that every child runs here. Upstream's fork (a new agent from a worker's built state) has no T3 equivalent, so that work goes to the worker itself with `t3_thread_send` (no rule covers it); checking on a worker uses `task_status`. Policy: the verifier runs on its worker's model and effort, not a different family, unless the user names another (rule same-model-review). Preserve one owner per PR, the never-edits-code rule, the three-level cap with about ten in flight as a rolling window, and starting with one unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Every worker runs on this machine. A worker that writes gets its own worktree (`isolation: "worktree"`), a checkout of your current local commit, with the kickoff naming the branch to check out first. Uncommitted edits are not copied, so commit first or name a pushed branch. A worker that only reads gets no `isolation` and shares your checkout. Runtime checks (the project's own verification skill, T3's `preview_*` tools, or the shell for a CLI), transcripts read with `t3_thread_read`, simulators, and auth that exists only here work from any worker. Set each subagent's effort for the job per the Subagents section, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Reuse a worker when new work needs its built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process: send the new work with `t3_thread_send` to its `childThreadId`. Otherwise message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent. Read its state with `task_status` instead.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the worker it checks, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.

=== entry b0d7f0bf067a
kind: translation
rules: stop, verify-surface
intent: Stopping a stalled worker is `task_cancel` (which stops what it delegated too), confirmed with `task_status`. The control-skill check becomes a check through the project's own verification skill. The term check catches neither, so this entry comes from review. Keep every other supervision rule verbatim.
--- upstream
- **Each kickoff names the evidence that proves the work is done,** such as a command and its expected output, a control-skill check, or an artifact. It also names the goal, the paths the worker may and may not write, and the actions it must not take. If you can't name the evidence, the unit isn't scoped yet.
- **Read the diff or the artifact, not only the worker's summary.** Green CI is an input to a verdict, not a verdict. Behavioral work needs more than a type check. A verdict covers one head SHA, so a new push needs a new verdict. Read a sample of each sub-coordinator's kickoffs while its workers run, and fix its instructions when one fails.
- **Send back work that drifts from scope or grows past what was asked.** Keep only the fixes that block the program, and park the rest as follow-ups.
- **Check a PR's live state before you report on it,** with `gh pr view` and `gh pr checks` or your forge's equivalents, not the worker's last report. Account for every worker you spawned as done, replaced, or absorbed into another worker's scope.
- **Stop or replace a worker that stalls or loops.** Judge it by its pushes and PR activity, not its status text. Retry by cause: a smaller scope after a context or memory limit, the same kickoff after a dropped connection, and a different model after a tool error. After two retries, drop the unit and replan around it. When a stopped worker returns late, check its result against the PR's current state, and hand any unique findings to a fresh worker.
- **Ask the user only for a real decision or a blocker,** such as an irreversible action, a product or preference call that no experiment settles, or a dead end that survived a replan. Rebase mechanics, retries, CI flakes, review triage, and "should I keep going" never reach the user.
--- patched
- **Each kickoff names the evidence that proves the work is done,** such as a command and its expected output, a check through the project's own verification skill, or an artifact. It also names the goal, the paths the worker may and may not write, and the actions it must not take. If you can't name the evidence, the unit isn't scoped yet.
- **Read the diff or the artifact, not only the worker's summary.** Green CI is an input to a verdict, not a verdict. Behavioral work needs more than a type check. A verdict covers one head SHA, so a new push needs a new verdict. Read a sample of each sub-coordinator's kickoffs while its workers run, and fix its instructions when one fails.
- **Send back work that drifts from scope or grows past what was asked.** Keep only the fixes that block the program, and park the rest as follow-ups.
- **Check a PR's live state before you report on it,** with `gh pr view` and `gh pr checks` or your forge's equivalents, not the worker's last report. Account for every worker you spawned as done, replaced, or absorbed into another worker's scope.
- **Stop or replace a worker that stalls or loops.** Stop it with `task_cancel`, which also stops the tasks it delegated, then confirm with `task_status`. Judge it by its pushes and PR activity, not its status text. Retry by cause: a smaller scope after a context or memory limit, the same kickoff after a dropped connection, and a different model after a tool error. After two retries, drop the unit and replan around it. When a stopped worker returns late, check its result against the PR's current state, and hand any unique findings to a fresh worker.
- **Ask the user only for a real decision or a blocker,** such as an irreversible action, a product or preference call that no experiment settles, or a dead end that survived a replan. Rebase mechanics, retries, CI flakes, review triage, and "should I keep going" never reach the user.
